(y)$ is as large as possible. \n\nThat is exactly what $\\operatorname{arg}⁡max$ returns.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y).\n$$"] ["question-0e94aa36-f8ac-5d68-8a85-88c135136bde" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d" :answer-field/id #uuid "e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d"] ["answer-eed8db63-444d-5be5-abc8-22b87e87b1b0" :answer/id #uuid "eed8db63-444d-5be5-abc8-22b87e87b1b0"] ["answer-eed8db63-444d-5be5-abc8-22b87e87b1b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-eed8db63-444d-5be5-abc8-22b87e87b1b0" :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)"] ["answer-450feb6f-7850-5f5d-8795-282d5fc86d84" :answer/id #uuid "450feb6f-7850-5f5d-8795-282d5fc86d84"] ["answer-450feb6f-7850-5f5d-8795-282d5fc86d84" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-450feb6f-7850-5f5d-8795-282d5fc86d84" :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)"] ["answer-e29d928e-baa8-5e76-b627-c742ea42fee2" :answer/id #uuid "e29d928e-baa8-5e76-b627-c742ea42fee2"] ["answer-e29d928e-baa8-5e76-b627-c742ea42fee2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e29d928e-baa8-5e76-b627-c742ea42fee2" :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{y\\in w(y)}\\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}"] ["answer-a3f54bd2-c0b3-56c8-b6df-6ffa0ee9341a" :answer/id #uuid "a3f54bd2-c0b3-56c8-b6df-6ffa0ee9341a"] ["answer-a3f54bd2-c0b3-56c8-b6df-6ffa0ee9341a" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a3f54bd2-c0b3-56c8-b6df-6ffa0ee9341a" :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}={max}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)"] ["answer-76db008e-9696-50d1-b034-aca1854d279f" :answer/id #uuid "76db008e-9696-50d1-b034-aca1854d279f"] ["answer-76db008e-9696-50d1-b034-aca1854d279f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-76db008e-9696-50d1-b034-aca1854d279f" :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}={min}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)"] ["answer-field-e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d" :answer-field/correct "answer-eed8db63-444d-5be5-abc8-22b87e87b1b0"] ["answer-field-e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d" :answer-field/choices "answer-eed8db63-444d-5be5-abc8-22b87e87b1b0"] ["answer-field-e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d" :answer-field/choices "answer-450feb6f-7850-5f5d-8795-282d5fc86d84"] ["answer-field-e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d" :answer-field/choices "answer-e29d928e-baa8-5e76-b627-c742ea42fee2"] ["answer-field-e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d" :answer-field/choices "answer-a3f54bd2-c0b3-56c8-b6df-6ffa0ee9341a"] ["answer-field-e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d" :answer-field/choices "answer-76db008e-9696-50d1-b034-aca1854d279f"] ["question-0e94aa36-f8ac-5d68-8a85-88c135136bde" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d"] [17592186231452 :knowledge-point/questions "question-0e94aa36-f8ac-5d68-8a85-88c135136bde"] [17592186232041 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we used $\\max$ and $\\min$ to talk about the largest and smallest *values* a function can take on a set.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn two closely related pieces of notation:\n\n- $\\max$ and $\\min$ return the best (largest/smallest) *function value*.\n\n- $\\arg\\max$ and $\\arg\\min$ return the *input value* (the \"argument\") where that best value occurs.\n\nFor example, let $S = \\{1,2,3\\}$ and suppose\n\n$$\nf(1)=4,\\qquad f(2)=7,\\qquad f(3)=6.\n$$\n\nThen the largest function value is $7,$ and it occurs when $x=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{max}_{x\\in S}f(x) & =7, \\\\ \\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in S}f(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the smallest function value, and $\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input where that smallest value occurs.\n\n**Watch out!** Sometimes the best value occurs at more than one input. In that case, we need to clarify what $\\arg\\max$ (or $\\arg\\min$) means. We will handle this after we practice the basic case."] [17592186232042 :tutorial/content "Sometimes a function attains its largest (or smallest) value at more than one input. In that case, there are multiple inputs that satisfy the definition of $\\arg\\max$ (or $\\arg\\min$).\n\nIn this course, when a tie occurs, we report the *set* of all inputs where the maximum is achieved as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x) = {x \\in S{:} f(x) = \\max_{u \\in S} f(u)}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when a tie occurs, we report the set of all inputs where the minimum is achieved as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x) = {x \\in S{:} f(x) = \\min_{u \\in S} f(u)}.\n$$\n\nThe quantities $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ and $\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ are still single numbers."] [17592186231551 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0]] "retractions" [[17592186231376 :question/problem "Let $S=\\{-1,0,2,4\\}$ and suppose\n$f(-1)=3,\\qquad f(0)=-2,\\qquad f(2)=5,\\qquad f(4)=1$.\nCompute the following:\n$\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\min_{x \\in S} f(x),\\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$."] [17592186231376 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n- $\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the largest output value, while\n- $\\arg\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that largest output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- $\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the smallest output value, while\n- $\\arg\\min\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that smallest output occurs.\n\nThe outputs of $f$ on $S$ are\n$3,\\qquad -2,\\qquad 5,\\qquad 1$.\nThe largest output is $5$, and it occurs at $x=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=5, \\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=2\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the smallest output is $-2$, and it occurs at $x=0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=-2, \\qquad \\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)=0\n$$"] [17592186231377 :question/problem "Let $S=\\{-1,0,2,3\\}$ and suppose\n$f(-1)=4,\\qquad f(0)=1,\\qquad f(2)=4,\\qquad f(3)=2$.\nCompute $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ and $\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$."] [17592186231377 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n- $\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the largest output value, while\n- $\\arg\\max\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that largest output occurs.\n\nThe output values are $4,1,4$, and $2$. The maximum output value is $4$.\n\nThis maximum occurs at both $x=-1$ and $x=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{4} \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\{-1,2\\}}\n$$"] [17592186231378 :question/problem "Let $S$ be a set and let $f$ be a real-valued function on $S$. Consider the expression\n$x^*=\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$.\nWhat does this statement mean in words? Also, what does $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ represent?"] [17592186231378 :question/worked-solution "The expression\n\n$$\nx^*=\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)\n$$\n\nmeans that:\n\n- $x^*$ is an input value in $S$.\n- Among all $x \\in S$, the value $f(x^*)$ is as large as possible.\n\nIn other words, $x^*$ is a choice of input where $f$ attains its largest value on $S$.\n\nBy contrast, $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is not an input. It is the largest *function value* that $f$ attains on $S$."] [17592186231742 :question/problem "Let $S = {2, 4, 6, 8}$ and suppose\n$g(2) = 5, g(4) =-3, g(6) = 10, g(8) = 0$.\nCompute the following:$max_(x ∈ S)g(x) =$\n{{field-1}} $\\arg max_(x ∈ S)g(x) =$\n{{field-2}} $min_(x ∈ S)g(x) =$\n{{field-3}} $\\arg min_(x ∈ S)g(x) =$\n{{field-4}}"] [17592186231735 :question/problem "Let $T = {-2,-1, 0, 1}$ and suppose\n$g(-2) = 3, g(-1) =-2, g(0) = 0, g(1) =-2$.\nCompute $min_(x ∈ T)g(x)$ and $\\arg min_(x ∈ T)g(x)$.\n$min_(x ∈ T)g(x) =$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\arg min_(x ∈ T)g(x) =$ {{field-2}}"] [17592186230997 :answer-field/choices 17592186230998] [17592186230997 :answer-field/choices 17592186230999] [17592186230997 :answer-field/choices 17592186231000] [17592186230997 :answer-field/choices 17592186231001] [17592186230997 :answer-field/choices 17592186231002] [17592186232041 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we used $\\max$ and $\\min$ to talk about the largest and smallest *values* a function can take on a set.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn two closely related pieces of notation:\n\n- $\\max$ and $\\min$ return the best (largest/smallest) *function value*.\n- $\\arg\\max$ and $\\arg\\min$ return the *input value* (the \"argument\") where that best value occurs.\n\nFor example, let\n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2,3\\}\n$$\n\nand suppose\n\n$$\nf(1)=4,\\qquad f(2)=7,\\qquad f(3)=6\n$$\n\nThen the largest function value is $7$, and it occurs when $x=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax_(x ∈ S)f(x) &= 7, \\\\\n\\arg max_(x ∈ S)f(x) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is the smallest function value, and $\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ is an input where that smallest value occurs.\n\n**Watch out!** Sometimes the best value occurs at more than one input. In that case, we need to clarify what $\\arg\\max$ (or $\\arg\\min$) means. We will handle this after we practice the basic case."] [17592186232042 :tutorial/content "Sometimes a function attains its largest (or smallest) value at more than one input. In that case, there are multiple inputs that satisfy the definition of $\\arg\\max$ (or $\\arg\\min$).\n\nIn this course, when a tie occurs, we report the *set* of all inputs where the maximum is achieved as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arg\\max_{x \\in S} f(x) = {x \\in S{:} f(x) = \\max_{u \\in S} f(u)}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when a tie occurs, we report the set of all inputs where the minimum is achieved as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arg\\min_{x \\in S} f(x) = {x \\in S{:} f(x) = \\min_{u \\in S} f(u)}\n$$\n\nThe quantities $\\max_{x \\in S} f(x)$ and $\\min_{x \\in S} f(x)$ are still single numbers."]]}]